.\" Man page for nic-report.py
.\" Written in classic troff/groff format
.TH NIC-REPORT 8 "2026-10-26" "nic-report 2.13.0" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
nic-report \- single- and multi-site NIC health reports from nic-sweep sweeps
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nic-report.py
.RI [ SWEEP_DIR ...]
.RI [ \-R | \-\-sweep\-root
.IR DIR ]
.RI [ \-s | \-\-site
.IR SITE ]...
.RI [ \-a | \-\-at
.IR DATE ]
.RI [ \-i | \-\-import
.I SITE PATH
.RI ...]...
.RI [ \-r | \-\-role\-regex
.IR RE ]
.RI [ \-b | \-\-baseline
.IR scope | all ]
.RI [ \-d | \-\-rx\-delta\-db
.IR DB ]
.RI [ \-f | \-\-flap\-min
.IR N ]
.RI [ \-O | \-\-output\-format
.IR md | json | html ]
.RI [ \-o | \-\-output
.IR FILE ]
.RI [ \-t | \-\-title
.IR TEXT ]
.RI [ \-c | \-\-classification
.IR TEXT ]
.RI [ \-v | \-\-version ]
.RI [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nic-report.py
reads the sweeps written by
.BR nic-sweep.sh (8)
(and, for older collections, nic-health CSV files) and writes a report for one
site, several sites, or every site of a sweep tree. The report has five
sections:
.TP
.B Coverage and comparability
Hosts, roles, NIC ASICs and ports, ports with link, deep checks evaluated, FEC
modes, nic-health versions, sampling windows and collection dates; every host
that was not collected; and caveats such as mixed tool versions, imported data
without collection metadata, or sites with no sweep at the requested date.
.TP
.B Verdicts
Hosts by verdict and the share of clean hosts, per site and per role.
.TP
.B Findings
Every WARN, CRIT and UNKNOWN result, worst first, grouped by site and host, with
its evidence and hint.
.TP
.B Baselines and outliers
Over ports with link: receive and transmit power per module type (minimum, p5
by nearest rank, median, maximum), receive-power outliers, ports near the
module's own low limit,
link errors since boot (with the rate per hour of uptime when known), ports with
many carrier changes, receive discards, the hottest modules and those near their
own high limit, PCIe links trained below what card and slot support, and
slot-limited cards. Ports whose module type has too few ports for a baseline are
counted as not evaluated.
.TP
.B Patterns
Within a site: the same issue on the same port or ASIC in two or more hosts of a
role (for example a PCIe link at Gen1 on 3 of 4 storage nodes); hosts count only
when collected. Across sites (several sites only): the same issue on the same
role at two or more sites, which hints at a shared cause such as a cabling
standard or a BIOS profile. Related checks form one issue (symbol, CRC and BER
errors are "link errors"; flaps and link-downs "link instability"). Without
.BR \-\-role\-regex ,
a site's hosts form one group and cross-site patterns are not computed; the
report says so.
.PP
With one site the coverage table has a column per role and the verdicts list
each host; with several sites the columns are sites. The tool is read-only and
offline: it never contacts a host or the network.
.PP
It is the one Python script of the nic-xray tools (Python 3.9 or newer,
standard library only).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I SWEEP_DIR
A sweep directory
.RI ( DIR / site / YYYYmmddTHHMMSSZ )
to report on as is.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-sweep\-root " " \fIDIR\fR
A nic-sweep tree. Selects the latest sweep of each site (or of each
.BR \-\-site ).
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-site " " \fISITE\fR
Only this site of the tree. Repeatable. An unknown site is an error that lists
the sites available.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-at " " \fIDATE\fR
Per site, the latest sweep on or before
.I DATE
(UTC):
.B YYYY-MM-DD
(end of that day) or
.BR YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM[:SS]Z .
Sites without such a sweep are listed as missing in the report.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-import " " "\fISITE PATH\fR ..."
Legacy nic-health CSV for
.IR SITE :
files, or directories of
.B *.csv
files. One file per host (host name taken from the file name up to the first
.B _
or
.BR . ),
or merged files with
.B site
and
.B hostname
columns. CRLF line endings, leading blank lines and fields wrapped by a terminal
capture are repaired. Imported data has no collection metadata, which the
report notes. A host may come from one file only, and a file that adds no row
for
.I SITE
is an error. Repeatable.
.B \-\-import
takes every following path: put
.I SWEEP_DIR
arguments before it, or end its list with
.BR \-\- .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-role\-regex " " \fIRE\fR
Derive each host's role from its name: the group named
.B role
(or the first group; a regex without a group is a usage error). Without it,
roles are empty.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-baseline " " \fIscope\fR|\fIall\fR
Where medians for outliers come from:
.B scope
(default) uses the sites in the report;
.B all
uses every site of
.B \-\-sweep\-root
(latest sweep each, at
.BR \-\-at ),
while the report still covers only the selection. Useful for one small site. A
tree site that cannot be read is left out of the baseline, and the report names
it and why.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-rx\-delta\-db " " \fIDB\fR
A port's receive power is an outlier when it is at least
.I DB
(default 5, a positive number) below the median of its module type, and its
robust score (median/MAD) is at least 3.5 away (in a group where all ports read
the same, the margin alone decides). A module type needs three ports with link
for a baseline; multi-lane modules form their own type, judged by their lowest
lane.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-flap\-min " " \fIN\fR
List ports with at least
.I N
(default 100) carrier changes since driver load.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-output\-format " " \fImd\fR|\fIjson\fR|\fIhtml\fR
Markdown (default), JSON with every computed value, or HTML: one self-contained
page (inline style, no scripts or remote resources; light and dark themes).
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write to
.I FILE
instead of standard output.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-title " " \fITEXT\fR
Report title (default: "NIC Health: SITE" or "NIC Health: cross-site
comparison (...)").
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-classification " " \fITEXT\fR
Classification label printed on the report.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help and exit.
.PP
A site may appear only once in a selection: a sweep directory and the same
site from
.B \-\-sweep\-root
is an error. Under
.BR \-\-sweep\-root ,
a sweep whose
.B sweep.json
names another site than its directory is an error. Symbolic links in the tree
are followed. Long options cannot be abbreviated.
.SH NOTES
Baselines use ports with link only: without light a module reads about
\-40 dBm and would distort them. For nic-health older than 2.12.0 (no link
state) link is inferred from a deep FEC mode plus light on the receiver, and the
report says for how many ports. Readings recorded in whole dBm (some vendor
tools round) are noted, since differences under 2 dB mean little there. The
module limits are the module's own
.RI [ low .. high ]
range as nic-health reports it. Errors since boot are uncorrectable symbol
errors.
A port counts as having link when nic-health reported its link state as
.BR up .
nic-health older than 2.12.0 records no link state: ports are then counted from
the link-flap records and ports with link are shown as unknown, with a caveat.
A collected host whose
.BI nodes/ host .json
is missing or malformed is listed with the hosts not collected (status
.BR unreadable-result )
instead of failing the report. The clean share is the share of collected hosts
whose verdict is OK.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Report written.
.TP
.B 1
Input error: unreadable input, an unsupported sweep schema, an unknown site, a
site selected twice, no data for the selection (for example
.B \-\-at
before every sweep); or the output file cannot be written.
.TP
.B 3
Usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Latest sweep of every site, roles from host names like prd-site-ctrl2d-01:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nic-report.py \-R sweeps \-\-role\-regex '^[^\-]+\-[^\-]+\-(?P<role>[a\-z]+\\d+[a\-z]*)\-\\d+'
.fi
.RE
.PP
One site as it was on a date, as JSON:
.PP
.RS
.B nic-report.py \-R sweeps \-s SITE1 \-a 2026-10-23 \-O json \-o site1.json
.RE
.PP
Mix a sweep tree with an older hand-made collection:
.PP
.RS
.B nic-report.py \-R sweeps \-i SITE9 old/SITE9/
.RE
.PP
One small site, outliers judged against the whole fleet, as an HTML page:
.PP
.RS
.B nic-report.py \-R sweeps \-s SITE3 \-b all \-O html \-o site3.html
.RE
